// CHAPTER 2: Circular Flow Model and Quantitative Elements (Term 1, Weeks 4-6)
// =============================================================================

blockNum = 0;
const ch2_lesson1 = [
  t(1, "## The Circular Flow Model\n\nThe circular flow model shows how money, goods, services, and factors of production flow between the different participants in the economy.\n\n### Participants in the Economy\n\n| Participant | Role |\n|-------------|------|\n| **Households** | Own factors of production; consume goods and services |\n| **Firms (businesses)** | Use factors of production to produce goods and services |\n| **Government** | Taxes, spends, regulates, and provides public services |\n| **Foreign sector** | Other countries that trade with South Africa |\n| **Financial sector** | Banks and institutions that channel savings into investment |\n\n### Two Types of Flows\n\n- **Real flows**: The physical movement of goods, services, and factors of production\n- **Money flows**: Payments for goods, services, and factors (wages, rent, interest, profit)\n\n### Two Markets\n\n- **Factor market**: Where households sell factors of production to firms (labour market, capital market)\n- **Goods market (product market)**: Where firms sell goods and services to households"),
  t(2, "### The Simple Two-Sector Model (Closed Economy)\n\nThe simplest circular flow has just two participants: **households** and **firms**.\n\n**Flow 1 — Factor market:**\n- Households supply factors of production (land, labour, capital, entrepreneurship) to firms\n- Firms pay factor incomes to households (rent, wages, interest, profit)\n\n**Flow 2 — Goods market:**\n- Firms produce goods and services and sell them to households\n- Households pay for goods and services with their income\n\nThis creates a continuous circular flow:\n- Money flows from firms to households as income, and back from households to firms as spending\n- Real goods and services flow from firms to households, while real factors flow from households to firms\n\n**Important assumption:** In the two-sector model, all income is spent (no saving). This means the total value of production equals total income, which equals total spending.\n\n**Total production = Total income = Total spending**\n\nThis relationship is the basis for measuring GDP."),
  q(3, "In the circular flow model, households supply ___ to firms through the factor market.",
    ["Goods and services", "Money", "Factors of production", "Taxes"], 2,
    "Households own the factors of production (land, labour, capital, entrepreneurship) and supply them to firms through the factor market. In return, they receive factor incomes."),
  t(4, "### Adding Government — The Three-Sector Model\n\nWhen we add government to the model, new flows appear:\n\n**Government and households:**\n- Government collects taxes from households (income tax, VAT)\n- Government pays social grants and provides public services (education, healthcare)\n- Government employs public servants (teachers, police, nurses)\n\n**Government and firms:**\n- Government collects taxes from firms (company tax, VAT)\n- Government buys goods and services from firms\n- Government provides infrastructure and subsidies\n\n**Injections and leakages:**\n- **Taxes (T)** are a **leakage** — money flows out of the circular flow to government\n- **Government spending (G)** is an **injection** — money flows back into the circular flow\n\nIn South Africa, government plays a major role in the economy:\n- Over 18 million people receive SASSA social grants\n- Government is the largest single employer\n- Government spending accounts for approximately 30% of GDP"),
  fb(5, "Taxes are a ___ from the circular flow, while government spending is an ___.",
    ["leakage", "injection"],
    "Leakages remove money from the circular flow (savings, taxes, imports). Injections put money back in (investment, government spending, exports)."),
  t(6, "### The Four-Sector Open Economy Model\n\nAdding the **foreign sector** creates the complete model, which represents South Africa as an open economy.\n\n**Exports (X):**\n- SA sells goods and services to other countries\n- Major exports: minerals (gold, platinum, iron ore, coal), vehicles, agricultural products (citrus, wine), services (tourism)\n- Exports are an **injection** — foreign money enters the economy\n\n**Imports (M):**\n- SA buys goods and services from other countries\n- Major imports: crude oil, electronics, machinery, vehicles, pharmaceuticals\n- Imports are a **leakage** — money leaves the economy\n\n**The financial sector:**\n- Banks and financial institutions channel savings (S) from households into investment (I) by firms\n- **Savings (S)** are a **leakage** — money is withdrawn from spending\n- **Investment (I)** is an **injection** — money flows back into production\n\n### Summary of Injections and Leakages\n\n| Injections | Leakages |\n|-----------|----------|\n| Investment (I) | Savings (S) |\n| Government spending (G) | Taxes (T) |\n| Exports (X) | Imports (M) |"),
  q(7, "Which of the following is an injection into the circular flow?",
    ["Savings", "Taxes", "Imports", "Exports"], 3,
    "Exports bring foreign money into the domestic economy, making them an injection. Savings, taxes, and imports are all leakages because they remove money from the circular flow."),
  t(8, "### Variables in the Circular Flow\n\n**Total production (Y)** can be measured from three perspectives:\n\n**1. Production approach (GDP):**\nGDP = Total value of all goods and services produced\n\n**2. Income approach (GDI):**\nGDI = Rent + Wages + Interest + Profit (total factor incomes)\n\n**3. Expenditure approach (GDE):**\nGDE = C + I + G + (X - M)\n\nWhere:\n- C = Consumption expenditure (household spending)\n- I = Investment (spending on capital goods)\n- G = Government spending\n- X = Exports\n- M = Imports\n- (X - M) = Net exports\n\n**In theory: GDP = GDI = GDE**\n\n### Equilibrium\n\nThe economy is in equilibrium when:\n\n**Total Injections = Total Leakages**\n\n**I + G + X = S + T + M**\n\n- If injections > leakages: the economy **expands** (more spending, more production, more jobs)\n- If leakages > injections: the economy **contracts** (less spending, less production, job losses)"),
  fb(9, "GDP measured by the expenditure method equals C + I + G + (X - ___). The economy is in equilibrium when total injections equal total ___.",
    ["M", "leakages"],
    "GDE = C + I + G + (X - M), where (X - M) is net exports. Equilibrium requires I + G + X = S + T + M."),
  q(10, "If total leakages exceed total injections, the economy will:",
    ["Expand", "Contract", "Stay the same", "Experience inflation"], 1,
    "When leakages (S + T + M) exceed injections (I + G + X), more money is being withdrawn from the circular flow than is being put back in. This causes the economy to contract — production falls, unemployment rises."),
];

blockNum = 0;
const ch2_lesson2 = [
  t(1, "## Quantitative Elements: GDP and GNI\n\n### Gross Domestic Product (GDP)\n\n**GDP** is the total monetary value of all final goods and services produced within a country's borders during a specific period (usually one year).\n\nKey points:\n- **Gross** = before deducting depreciation (wear and tear of capital goods)\n- **Domestic** = within the country's borders (regardless of who owns the factors)\n- **Product** = goods and services produced\n- Only **final** goods are counted (not intermediate goods, to avoid double-counting)\n\n**Example of avoiding double-counting:**\nA wheat farmer sells flour to a bakery for R10. The bakery sells bread for R25. Only R25 (the final good) is counted in GDP, not R10 + R25 = R35.\n\nAlternatively, we count the **value added** at each stage:\n- Farmer adds R10 of value\n- Bakery adds R15 of value (R25 - R10)\n- Total value added = R10 + R15 = R25 = GDP"),
  t(2, "### Nominal vs Real GDP\n\n**Nominal GDP** is measured at current prices. It changes due to both changes in production and changes in prices (inflation).\n\n**Real GDP** is measured at constant prices (adjusted for inflation). It shows the true change in production.\n\n**Example:**\n\n| Year | Quantity | Price | Nominal GDP | Real GDP (base year prices) |\n|------|----------|-------|-------------|---------------------------|\n| 2023 (base) | 100 units | R10 | R1 000 | R1 000 |\n| 2024 | 100 units | R12 | R1 200 | R1 000 |\n| 2025 | 110 units | R12 | R1 320 | R1 100 |\n\nIn 2024, nominal GDP rose by 20%, but real GDP stayed the same — the increase was only due to inflation, not more production. In 2025, real GDP rose by 10%, showing actual economic growth.\n\n**Real GDP is the better measure** of economic growth because it removes the effect of price changes.\n\n**GDP growth rate** = ((Real GDP this year - Real GDP last year) / Real GDP last year) x 100"),
  q(3, "Why is real GDP preferred over nominal GDP when measuring economic growth?",
    ["It is easier to calculate", "It removes the effect of inflation", "It includes imports and exports", "It measures wealth distribution"], 1,
    "Real GDP uses constant base-year prices, removing the distortion caused by inflation. Nominal GDP can rise even when production stays the same, simply because prices increased."),
  t(4, "### Gross National Income (GNI)\n\n**GNI** measures the total income earned by a country's residents, regardless of where they earn it.\n\n**GNI = GDP + Income earned abroad by SA residents - Income earned in SA by foreign residents**\n\nOr: GNI = GDP + Net factor income from abroad\n\n**Difference between GDP and GNI:**\n- GDP measures production within SA borders (includes output by foreign firms in SA)\n- GNI measures income of SA residents (includes income SA citizens earn abroad, excludes income foreigners earn in SA)\n\n**Example:**\n- A German company (BMW) produces cars in Rosslyn, Pretoria. This output is part of SA GDP but the profits sent to Germany are excluded from SA GNI.\n- A South African working in London earns income that is part of SA GNI but not SA GDP.\n\n**Per capita income:**\nGDP per capita = GDP / Population\nGNI per capita = GNI / Population\n\nSA GDP per capita is approximately R84 000. However, this average is misleading because income is extremely unequally distributed (Gini coefficient of 0.63)."),
  fb(5, "GDP measures the value of production within a country's ___, while GNI measures the total income earned by a country's ___.",
    ["borders", "residents"],
    "GDP is location-based (where production happens). GNI is ownership-based (who earns the income). The difference depends on net factor income from abroad."),
  t(6, "### Limitations of GDP\n\nGDP is the most widely used measure of economic activity, but it has important limitations:\n\n1. **Does not measure inequality**: GDP per capita is an average that hides how income is distributed. SA has high GDP per capita for Africa but extreme poverty.\n\n2. **Excludes the informal economy**: Activities in the informal sector (street vendors, domestic workers paid in cash) are not fully captured. SA's informal economy is estimated at 5-12% of GDP.\n\n3. **Excludes non-market activities**: Unpaid housework, subsistence farming, volunteer work, and caring for family members are not counted.\n\n4. **Ignores environmental damage**: GDP counts mining output as positive but does not subtract the environmental cost (pollution, resource depletion).\n\n5. **Does not measure quality of life**: GDP does not capture crime levels, health, happiness, leisure time, or political freedom.\n\n6. **Excludes the underground economy**: Illegal activities (drug trade, smuggling) are not measured.\n\n**Alternative measures:**\n- **Human Development Index (HDI)**: Combines health, education, and income\n- **Gini coefficient**: Measures income inequality\n- **Green GDP**: Adjusts for environmental damage"),
  q(7, "Which of the following activities would NOT be included in GDP?",
    ["A teacher working at a government school", "A factory producing clothes for export", "A mother cooking dinner for her family at home", "A shopkeeper selling groceries"], 2,
    "Unpaid household work like cooking for your own family is not included in GDP because no market transaction takes place. All the other options involve market-based economic activity."),
  t(8, "### The Flows of Variables\n\nUnderstanding the difference between **stocks** and **flows** is important:\n\n**Stock variable**: Measured at a specific point in time\n- Wealth (total assets owned at a given date)\n- Population (number of people at a specific date)\n- Money supply (amount of money in the economy at a point in time)\n- Capital stock (total machinery, buildings, etc. at a point in time)\n\n**Flow variable**: Measured over a period of time\n- GDP (total production over one year)\n- Income (earnings over a month or year)\n- Investment (spending on capital goods during a year)\n- Consumption (spending on goods and services during a period)\n\n**Example:** A household's bank balance is a stock (R50 000 on 1 January). The salary earned during January is a flow (R25 000 per month).\n\nGDP is a flow variable — it measures the total output over a specific period. Wealth is a stock variable — it measures the total value of assets at a specific moment."),
  fb(9, "GDP is a ___ variable because it is measured over a period of time. Wealth is a ___ variable because it is measured at a point in time.",
    ["flow", "stock"],
    "The distinction between stocks and flows is fundamental in economics. Income (a flow) adds to wealth (a stock), while spending reduces it."),
  q(10, "Per capita GDP is calculated by dividing GDP by:",
    ["The number of firms", "Total government spending", "The population", "The inflation rate"], 2,
    "Per capita GDP = GDP / Population. It gives the average GDP per person, but does not show how income is actually distributed among the population."),
];

