blockNum = 0;
const ch3_lesson1 = [
  t(1, "## The Business Cycle\n\nThe **business cycle** refers to the recurring pattern of expansion (growth) and contraction (decline) in economic activity over time. No economy grows at a constant rate — there are periods of boom and periods of recession.\n\n### The Phases of the Business Cycle\n\nThe business cycle has four main phases:\n\n| Phase | Description |\n|-------|-------------|\n| **Upswing (Recovery/Expansion)** | Economic activity increases; GDP grows; employment rises; consumer confidence improves |\n| **Peak (Boom/Prosperity)** | Economic activity reaches its highest point; full employment approached; inflation pressure increases |\n| **Downswing (Contraction/Recession)** | Economic activity decreases; GDP falls; unemployment rises; business confidence drops |\n| **Trough (Depression)** | Economic activity reaches its lowest point; high unemployment; low consumer spending; many businesses fail |\n\nAfter the trough, the economy begins a new upswing, and the cycle repeats.\n\n**A recession** is technically defined as two or more consecutive quarters (6 months) of negative real GDP growth."),
  t(2, "### Characteristics of Each Phase\n\n**Upswing:**\n- Rising production and sales\n- Falling unemployment\n- Increasing consumer and business confidence\n- Rising investment in new projects\n- Increasing demand for credit (loans)\n- Prices begin to rise as demand increases\n\n**Peak:**\n- High levels of production and employment\n- Strong consumer spending\n- Inflation accelerates (demand-pull inflation)\n- Shortages of skilled workers and resources\n- Interest rates may rise to control inflation\n- Economy operating at or near full capacity\n\n**Downswing:**\n- Falling production and sales\n- Rising unemployment\n- Declining consumer and business confidence\n- Falling investment\n- Businesses cut costs (retrenchments, budget cuts)\n- Prices stabilise or fall\n\n**Trough:**\n- Low production and high unemployment\n- Very low consumer confidence\n- Many business closures and bankruptcies\n- Government typically intervenes with stimulus measures\n- Low interest rates to encourage recovery"),
  q(3, "A recession is officially defined as:",
    ["One quarter of negative GDP growth", "Two or more consecutive quarters of negative real GDP growth", "A year of rising unemployment", "Inflation above 6%"], 1,
    "A recession is technically defined as two or more consecutive quarters (6 months) of negative real GDP growth. This is the standard international definition used by economists."),
  t(4, "### Indicators of the Business Cycle\n\nEconomists use various indicators to track where the economy is in the business cycle:\n\n**Leading indicators** (predict future economic activity):\n- Building plans approved (construction activity to come)\n- New vehicle sales\n- Share prices on the JSE\n- Business confidence index\n- Job advertisements\n\n**Coincident indicators** (move with current economic activity):\n- GDP growth rate\n- Industrial production\n- Retail sales\n- Employment levels\n\n**Lagging indicators** (confirm past trends):\n- Unemployment rate (takes time to respond to economic changes)\n- Inflation rate\n- Interest rates\n- Number of insolvencies (bankruptcies)\n\nIn South Africa, the **South African Reserve Bank (SARB)** publishes a composite leading, coincident, and lagging business cycle indicator monthly. These help government and businesses plan for future economic conditions."),
  fb(5, "The four phases of the business cycle are upswing, ___, downswing, and ___.",
    ["peak", "trough"],
    "The business cycle moves from upswing to peak, then downswing to trough, before beginning a new upswing. This pattern repeats over time, though cycles vary in length and intensity."),
  t(6, "### South African Business Cycles\n\nSA has experienced several notable business cycles:\n\n**Recent history:**\n- **2008-2009**: The Global Financial Crisis caused SA's first recession since 1992. GDP contracted by 1.5% in 2009.\n- **2010-2013**: Recovery driven partly by the FIFA World Cup infrastructure spending, but growth remained modest (2-3%).\n- **2014-2019**: Low growth period (0.5-1.5%) due to load shedding, policy uncertainty, state capture, and global commodity price declines.\n- **2020**: COVID-19 pandemic caused the worst recession in SA history. GDP contracted by 6.4%. Lockdowns devastated the hospitality, retail, and informal sectors.\n- **2021-2022**: Partial recovery, but growth was weak. Load shedding worsened.\n\n**Time-series composition:**\nA time series of GDP data can be decomposed into:\n1. **Trend**: The long-term direction of the economy (generally upward in growing economies)\n2. **Cyclical pattern**: The business cycle fluctuations around the trend\n3. **Seasonal pattern**: Regular variations within a year (e.g., retail peaks in December)\n4. **Random/irregular variations**: Unpredictable events (natural disasters, pandemics)"),
  q(7, "South Africa's GDP contracted by 6.4% in 2020. This was primarily caused by:",
    ["The Global Financial Crisis", "Load shedding", "The COVID-19 pandemic and lockdowns", "A drought in the Western Cape"], 2,
    "The COVID-19 pandemic and the associated lockdown restrictions caused the worst economic contraction in SA's modern history. Many businesses were forced to close, and millions lost their income."),
  t(8, "### Exogenous and Endogenous Causes\n\nBusiness cycles are caused by both internal and external factors:\n\n**Endogenous causes** (originate within the economy):\n- Changes in consumer and business confidence\n- Fluctuations in investment spending\n- Government fiscal policy changes (tax increases or spending cuts)\n- Changes in money supply and interest rates\n- Inventory cycles (firms build up and run down stock)\n- Load shedding reducing production capacity\n\n**Exogenous causes** (originate outside the economy):\n- Global recessions (2008 Global Financial Crisis)\n- Pandemics (COVID-19 in 2020)\n- Changes in global commodity prices (oil, gold, platinum)\n- Wars and geopolitical tensions (Russia-Ukraine conflict affecting fuel and food prices)\n- Natural disasters (droughts, floods)\n- Changes in international trade policy (tariffs, sanctions)\n\n**The multiplier effect** amplifies the impact: an initial change in spending creates a ripple effect through the economy, making both upswings and downswings larger than the initial shock."),
  fb(9, "Endogenous causes of business cycles originate ___ the economy, while exogenous causes originate ___ the economy.",
    ["within", "outside"],
    "Load shedding is an endogenous cause (internal to SA), while a global recession is an exogenous cause (external shock). Both types can trigger or worsen business cycle fluctuations."),
  q(10, "Which of the following is a leading indicator of the business cycle?",
    ["The current unemployment rate", "Last year's inflation figure", "Building plans approved", "The number of insolvencies"], 2,
    "Building plans approved are a leading indicator because they predict future construction activity and economic growth. Unemployment and insolvencies are lagging indicators. Inflation can be both coincident and lagging."),
];

blockNum = 0;
const ch3_lesson2 = [
  t(1, "## Effects of the Business Cycle\n\nThe business cycle affects every part of the economy and society:\n\n### Impact on Households\n\n**During upswing/boom:**\n- More job opportunities and lower unemployment\n- Rising wages and incomes\n- Increased consumer confidence and spending\n- Higher standard of living\n- Greater access to credit\n\n**During downswing/recession:**\n- Job losses and rising unemployment\n- Falling incomes and financial stress\n- Reduced consumer confidence and spending\n- Increased poverty and inequality\n- Defaults on loans and debt\n- Mental health and social problems increase\n\n### Impact on Businesses\n\n**During upswing/boom:**\n- Higher sales and revenue\n- Increased investment and expansion\n- Higher profits\n- Difficulty finding skilled workers (labour shortages)\n\n**During downswing/recession:**\n- Falling sales and revenue\n- Retrenchments and cost-cutting\n- Business closures and insolvencies\n- Reduced investment\n- Excess capacity (underutilised factories and equipment)"),
  t(2, "### Impact on Government\n\n**During upswing/boom:**\n- Tax revenue increases (more income tax, company tax, VAT collected)\n- Social grant spending may decrease as unemployment falls\n- Government can reduce borrowing or pay off debt\n- Resources available for infrastructure and development\n\n**During downswing/recession:**\n- Tax revenue falls (lower incomes, fewer companies profitable)\n- Social grant applications increase\n- Government must borrow more, increasing the national debt\n- Pressure to stimulate the economy through spending\n\n### Cyclical Patterns in SA\n\nSouth Africa's business cycle is influenced by:\n1. **Global commodity prices** — SA is a major exporter of gold, platinum, and coal. When global prices fall, the mining sector contracts.\n2. **Electricity supply** — Load shedding disrupts production across all sectors.\n3. **Political stability** — Policy uncertainty reduces investor confidence.\n4. **Interest rates** — The SARB adjusts rates to manage inflation and growth.\n5. **Global economic conditions** — SA's open economy is affected by growth or recession in major trading partners (China, EU, USA)."),
  q(3, "During an economic recession, what typically happens to government tax revenue?",
    ["It increases because government raises tax rates", "It decreases because incomes and profits fall", "It stays the same because tax rates are fixed", "It doubles because more people pay VAT"], 1,
    "During a recession, fewer people are employed (less income tax), businesses earn lower profits (less company tax), and consumers spend less (less VAT). This reduces total tax revenue even if tax rates stay the same."),
  t(4, "### Government Responses to the Business Cycle\n\n**Counter-cyclical fiscal policy:**\nGovernment adjusts its spending and taxation to smooth out the business cycle.\n\n**During recession (expansionary policy):**\n- Increase government spending on infrastructure projects\n- Reduce taxes to boost consumer spending\n- Expand social safety nets (grants, unemployment insurance)\n- Fund public works programmes (EPWP) to create temporary jobs\n\n**During boom (contractionary policy):**\n- Reduce government spending to prevent overheating\n- Increase taxes to cool demand\n- Build up budget surpluses for future downturns\n\n**Monetary policy (SARB):**\n- **During recession**: Lower the repo rate to make borrowing cheaper, encourage spending and investment\n- **During boom**: Raise the repo rate to control inflation and prevent the economy from overheating\n\n**SA policy responses during COVID-19 (2020):**\n- R500 billion fiscal stimulus package\n- Special COVID-19 Social Relief of Distress grant (R350/month)\n- Loan guarantee scheme for businesses\n- SARB cut the repo rate by 300 basis points (from 6.25% to 3.5%)"),
  fb(5, "Counter-cyclical fiscal policy means the government ___ spending during recessions and ___ spending during booms.",
    ["increases", "decreases"],
    "By spending more during downturns and less during booms, government aims to stabilise the economy and reduce the severity of business cycle fluctuations."),
  t(6, "### The Multiplier Effect\n\nThe **multiplier** shows how an initial injection of spending creates a larger total increase in national income through successive rounds of spending.\n\n**How it works:**\n1. Government spends R10 million building a road (initial injection)\n2. Construction workers earn R10 million in wages\n3. Workers spend R8 million on goods and services (saving R2 million)\n4. Shopkeepers earn R8 million and spend R6.4 million\n5. This continues through many rounds...\n\n**The multiplier formula:**\n\n**k = 1 / (1 - MPC)** or **k = 1 / MPS**\n\nWhere:\n- MPC = Marginal Propensity to Consume (the fraction of each extra rand that is spent)\n- MPS = Marginal Propensity to Save (the fraction of each extra rand that is saved)\n- MPC + MPS = 1\n\n**Example:** If MPC = 0.8:\n- MPS = 1 - 0.8 = 0.2\n- k = 1 / 0.2 = 5\n- An injection of R10 million increases national income by R10m x 5 = **R50 million**"),
  q(7, "If the MPC is 0.75, what is the value of the multiplier?",
    ["0.75", "0.25", "4", "3"], 2,
    "k = 1 / (1 - MPC) = 1 / (1 - 0.75) = 1 / 0.25 = 4. Each rand of new spending will ultimately generate R4 of additional national income."),
  t(8, "### Practical Multiplier Considerations\n\n**Why the multiplier is smaller in practice:**\n\nThe simple multiplier assumes the only leakage is savings. In reality, there are three leakages:\n- Savings (S)\n- Taxes (T)\n- Imports (M)\n\nThese all reduce the amount respent in each round, making the actual multiplier smaller.\n\nSA has a relatively small multiplier (estimated at 1.2 to 2.0) because:\n- High import levels (many consumer goods are imported)\n- Significant tax rates (income tax, VAT)\n- The savings rate, while low, still constitutes a leakage\n\n**Negative multiplier:**\nThe multiplier also works in reverse. A withdrawal of R10 million from the economy will cause national income to fall by R10m x k. This is why recessions can deepen quickly.\n\n**Example:** During the 2020 COVID-19 lockdown, reduced consumer spending created a negative multiplier effect. Each rand of lost spending caused a larger total decline in economic activity, leading to widespread job losses and business closures."),
  fb(9, "The multiplier formula is k = 1 / (1 - ___). If the MPC is 0.5, the multiplier equals ___.",
    ["MPC", "2"],
    "k = 1 / (1 - 0.5) = 1 / 0.5 = 2. A lower MPC means more money leaks out at each round, giving a smaller multiplier."),
  q(10, "Government invests R20 million in a new school. The multiplier is 3. What is the total increase in national income?",
    ["R20 million", "R40 million", "R60 million", "R23 million"], 2,
    "Total increase = Initial injection x Multiplier = R20 million x 3 = R60 million. The initial spending creates successive rounds of income and spending that multiply the original amount."),
];

