blockNum = 0;
const ch4_lesson1 = [
  t(1, "## Markets and Prices\n\nA **market** is any arrangement where buyers and sellers come together to exchange goods and services. Markets can be physical (a shopping mall, a fruit market) or virtual (online shopping, the JSE).\n\n### Value, Price, and Utility\n\n**Value** has two meanings in economics:\n- **Value in use**: The satisfaction a good gives when consumed (water has high value in use)\n- **Value in exchange**: What a good can be traded for (diamonds have high value in exchange)\n\nAdam Smith's **diamond-water paradox**: Water is essential for life (high value in use) but cheap (low value in exchange). Diamonds are non-essential (low value in use) but expensive (high value in exchange). This paradox is explained by **scarcity** — water is abundant while diamonds are rare.\n\n**Price** is the amount of money paid for a good or service. Prices are determined by the interaction of demand and supply in the market.\n\n**Utility** is the satisfaction or benefit a consumer gets from consuming a good or service.\n- **Total utility**: The total satisfaction from consuming all units\n- **Marginal utility**: The additional satisfaction from consuming one more unit\n- **Law of diminishing marginal utility**: Each additional unit consumed gives less additional satisfaction"),
  t(2, "## Demand\n\n**Demand** is the quantity of a good or service that consumers are willing and able to buy at various prices during a specific period, ceteris paribus.\n\n### The Law of Demand\n\nAs the price of a good increases, the quantity demanded decreases, ceteris paribus. As the price decreases, the quantity demanded increases.\n\nThis creates a **downward-sloping demand curve** on a graph (price on the y-axis, quantity on the x-axis).\n\n**Why does the demand curve slope downward?**\n1. **Income effect**: When price rises, your money buys less (real income falls), so you buy less\n2. **Substitution effect**: When price rises, you switch to cheaper alternatives\n3. **Diminishing marginal utility**: Each extra unit gives less satisfaction, so you only buy more at lower prices\n\n**Individual demand** = one consumer's demand at each price\n**Market demand** = the total of all individual demands at each price (add all quantities at each price)"),
  q(3, "The law of demand states that, ceteris paribus:",
    ["Price and quantity demanded move in the same direction", "Price and quantity demanded move in opposite directions", "Demand always increases over time", "Higher prices lead to higher demand"], 1,
    "The law of demand shows an inverse (opposite) relationship between price and quantity demanded. When price goes up, quantity demanded goes down, and vice versa. This is shown by the downward-sloping demand curve."),
  t(4, "### Factors that Shift the Demand Curve\n\nA change in **price** causes a movement **along** the demand curve (change in quantity demanded).\n\nA change in **other factors** causes a **shift** of the entire demand curve:\n\n| Factor | Demand increases (shifts right) | Demand decreases (shifts left) |\n|--------|-------------------------------|-------------------------------|\n| **Income** | Income rises (for normal goods) | Income falls (for normal goods) |\n| **Price of substitutes** | Substitute becomes more expensive | Substitute becomes cheaper |\n| **Price of complements** | Complement becomes cheaper | Complement becomes more expensive |\n| **Tastes and preferences** | Product becomes trendy or popular | Product falls out of fashion |\n| **Population** | Population grows | Population shrinks |\n| **Expectations** | Consumers expect price to rise (buy now) | Consumers expect price to fall (wait) |\n| **Season** | Product is in season (ice cream in summer) | Product is out of season |\n\n**Normal goods**: Demand increases when income increases (most goods)\n**Inferior goods**: Demand decreases when income increases (cheap substitutes like no-name brands)"),
  fb(5, "A change in price causes a movement ___ the demand curve. A change in income or tastes causes a ___ of the demand curve.",
    ["along", "shift"],
    "This distinction is crucial. Price changes move you along the existing curve. Non-price factors shift the entire curve to the left or right."),
  t(6, "## Supply\n\n**Supply** is the quantity of a good or service that producers are willing and able to offer for sale at various prices during a specific period, ceteris paribus.\n\n### The Law of Supply\n\nAs the price of a good increases, the quantity supplied increases, ceteris paribus. As the price decreases, the quantity supplied decreases.\n\nThis creates an **upward-sloping supply curve** on a graph.\n\n**Why does the supply curve slope upward?** Higher prices mean higher potential profits, which motivate producers to supply more.\n\n### Factors that Shift the Supply Curve\n\n| Factor | Supply increases (shifts right) | Supply decreases (shifts left) |\n|--------|-------------------------------|-------------------------------|\n| **Input costs** | Costs fall (cheaper raw materials, lower wages) | Costs rise (fuel, electricity, wages) |\n| **Technology** | Better technology reduces production costs | Technology breakdown |\n| **Government policy** | Subsidies, deregulation | Taxes, stricter regulations |\n| **Number of producers** | New firms enter the market | Firms exit the market |\n| **Natural conditions** | Good weather (for agriculture) | Drought, floods, disasters |\n| **Expectations** | Expected lower future prices (sell now) | Expected higher future prices (hold stock) |"),
  q(7, "If the cost of electricity (an input) increases significantly, the supply curve will:",
    ["Shift to the right", "Shift to the left", "Not change", "Become vertical"], 1,
    "Higher input costs increase the cost of production, making it less profitable to supply goods at each price. This shifts the supply curve to the left (decrease in supply), meaning less is supplied at every price level."),
  t(8, "## Market Equilibrium\n\n**Market equilibrium** occurs where the demand curve and the supply curve intersect. At this point:\n- The **equilibrium price (Pe)** is determined\n- The **equilibrium quantity (Qe)** is determined\n- There is no surplus or shortage\n\n### What Happens Away from Equilibrium?\n\n**Price above equilibrium (P > Pe):**\n- Quantity supplied > Quantity demanded\n- **Surplus** (excess supply) exists\n- Producers lower prices to sell excess stock\n- Price falls back toward equilibrium\n\n**Price below equilibrium (P < Pe):**\n- Quantity demanded > Quantity supplied\n- **Shortage** (excess demand) exists\n- Consumers bid up prices to secure scarce goods\n- Price rises back toward equilibrium\n\nThe market automatically adjusts toward equilibrium through the **price mechanism** — what Adam Smith called the **invisible hand**.\n\n**Example in SA:** When there is a petrol shortage (supply disruption), prices rise. When there is an oversupply of maize after a good harvest, prices fall. The market naturally moves toward equilibrium."),
  fb(9, "Market equilibrium occurs where the demand curve ___ the supply curve. A surplus exists when price is ___ equilibrium.",
    ["intersects", "above"],
    "At the intersection point, quantity demanded equals quantity supplied. A surplus (excess supply) occurs when price is too high, and a shortage (excess demand) occurs when price is too low."),
  q(10, "A severe drought destroys much of the maize crop. What happens to equilibrium price and quantity?",
    ["Price rises, quantity rises", "Price falls, quantity falls", "Price rises, quantity falls", "Price falls, quantity rises"], 2,
    "The drought reduces supply (supply curve shifts left). At the new equilibrium, the price is higher (maize is scarcer and more expensive) and the quantity traded is lower (less maize is available)."),
];

blockNum = 0;
const ch4_lesson2 = [
  t(1, "## Perfect Markets and Imperfect Markets\n\n### Perfect Market (Perfect Competition)\n\nA **perfect market** is a theoretical model with the following characteristics:\n\n1. **Many buyers and many sellers** — No single buyer or seller can influence the price\n2. **Homogeneous products** — All firms sell identical products\n3. **Perfect information** — All buyers and sellers have full knowledge of prices and products\n4. **Free entry and exit** — Firms can easily enter or leave the market\n5. **No transport costs** — Products are easily available everywhere\n6. **Price takers** — Firms and consumers accept the market price\n\nPerfect markets are rare in reality. The closest examples might be agricultural commodity markets (maize, wheat) where the product is standardised and there are many producers.\n\n### Functions of Markets\n\nMarkets serve several important functions:\n- **Allocate resources** through the price mechanism\n- **Distribute income** through factor markets\n- **Signal information** through prices (high price = scarcity)\n- **Provide incentives** for producers (profit motive)\n- **Promote efficiency** through competition"),
  t(2, "### Imperfect Markets\n\nMost real-world markets are **imperfect**, meaning they do not meet all the conditions of a perfect market.\n\n**Types of imperfect markets:**\n\n| Market structure | Number of sellers | Product | Price influence | Entry barriers | SA example |\n|-----------------|------------------|---------|----------------|---------------|------------|\n| **Monopoly** | One seller | Unique product | Price maker (sets the price) | Very high | Eskom (electricity) |\n| **Oligopoly** | Few large sellers | Similar or differentiated | Significant (price leadership) | High | Cell networks (Vodacom, MTN, Cell C), Banks |\n| **Monopolistic competition** | Many sellers | Differentiated | Some (brand loyalty) | Low | Restaurants, clothing stores |\n\n**Monopoly:**\n- Single seller dominates the entire market\n- No close substitutes available\n- High barriers to entry (legal, technological, financial)\n- Can charge higher prices than a competitive market\n- SA examples: Eskom (electricity generation monopoly), Transnet (rail freight)\n\n**Oligopoly:**\n- A few large firms dominate the market\n- Actions of one firm directly affect others (interdependence)\n- May engage in collusion (price-fixing) — illegal under the Competition Act\n- SA examples: Cell networks, major banks, supermarket chains"),
  q(3, "Eskom is an example of which market structure?",
    ["Perfect competition", "Monopolistic competition", "Oligopoly", "Monopoly"], 3,
    "Eskom is a monopoly because it is the single dominant supplier of electricity in South Africa. It has no significant competitors in electricity generation and distribution, and barriers to entry are extremely high."),
  t(4, "### How Prices Are Formed\n\nIn a market economy, prices are determined by the interaction of demand and supply — the **price mechanism**.\n\n**Price formation process:**\n1. Buyers and sellers come to the market with different intentions\n2. Buyers want to pay as little as possible; sellers want to charge as much as possible\n3. Through negotiation and competition, a market price emerges\n4. This price allocates resources: goods go to those willing and able to pay\n\n**How prices allocate resources:**\n- **Rationing function**: Prices ensure that only those who value the good enough to pay the price will buy it\n- **Signalling function**: High prices signal to producers that a good is in demand (encourage more production). Low prices signal that a good is not in demand (discourage production).\n- **Incentive function**: High prices and profits attract new firms into the market; low prices and losses push firms out\n\n**Example:** During COVID-19, the price of face masks increased dramatically because demand surged while supply was limited. The high price signalled to producers to make more masks, and eventually supply increased and prices fell."),
  fb(5, "In a monopoly there is ___ seller. In an oligopoly there are a ___ large sellers.",
    ["one", "few"],
    "Market structure is defined by the number of sellers, the type of product, barriers to entry, and the degree of price influence each firm has."),
  t(6, "### The Role of the Price Mechanism\n\nThe **price mechanism** is the process by which prices in a free market coordinate the decisions of buyers and sellers to allocate resources.\n\n**How it works:**\n\n**Step 1 — Demand increases for a product:**\n- Consumers want more (demand shifts right)\n- At the current price, a shortage develops\n- Price rises\n\n**Step 2 — Higher price sends signals:**\n- To producers: \"Produce more of this — it is profitable\"\n- To consumers: \"This is expensive — buy less or find alternatives\"\n\n**Step 3 — Resources reallocated:**\n- Firms shift resources toward producing the high-demand product\n- New firms may enter the market\n- Supply increases until a new equilibrium is reached\n\n**Advantages of the price mechanism:**\n- Works automatically without central planning\n- Responds quickly to changes in demand and supply\n- Promotes efficiency through competition\n\n**Limitations:**\n- Cannot provide public goods (defence, street lights)\n- Does not account for externalities (pollution)\n- May lead to inequality\n- May be distorted by monopolies"),
  q(7, "The price mechanism allocates resources through the functions of:",
    ["Taxation and subsidies", "Rationing, signalling, and incentives", "Government planning boards", "Trade unions and employers"], 1,
    "The price mechanism allocates resources through three functions: rationing (limiting demand to those who can afford the price), signalling (communicating information about scarcity and demand), and incentives (motivating producers to adjust supply)."),
  t(8, "### World Markets and Electronics\n\nSouth Africa is part of the **global market** for many products, especially electronics.\n\n**How world markets affect SA:**\n- SA imports most of its electronics (smartphones, computers, TVs) from countries like China, South Korea, and Japan\n- The **exchange rate** (value of the rand against other currencies) affects import prices:\n  - Weak rand = imported electronics become more expensive for SA consumers\n  - Strong rand = imported electronics become cheaper\n- Global supply chain disruptions (e.g., semiconductor shortages in 2021-2022) increased electronics prices worldwide, including in SA\n\n**The electronics market is an oligopoly:**\n- A few large firms dominate: Apple, Samsung, Huawei, Xiaomi\n- Products are differentiated (branding, features)\n- High research and development costs create barriers to entry\n- Intense competition on innovation and features\n\n**Impact on SA consumers:**\n- Access to global technology and innovation\n- Prices affected by the rand exchange rate\n- Choice between premium and budget brands\n- Second-hand and refurbished markets growing as new devices are expensive"),
  fb(9, "When the rand weakens, imported goods become ___ expensive. When the rand strengthens, imported goods become ___.",
    ["more", "cheaper"],
    "The exchange rate directly affects the price of imports. A weaker rand means SA consumers need more rands to buy goods priced in dollars or euros, making imports more expensive."),
  q(10, "The SA cell phone market (Vodacom, MTN, Cell C, Telkom) is best described as:",
    ["Perfect competition", "Monopoly", "Oligopoly", "Monopolistic competition"], 2,
    "The SA cell phone market is an oligopoly because it is dominated by a few large firms (Vodacom, MTN, Cell C, Telkom) that are interdependent. Products are slightly differentiated, and high infrastructure costs create significant barriers to entry."),
];

