blockNum = 0;
const ch5_lesson1 = [
  t(1, "## The Public Sector and Government Intervention\n\nThe **public sector** consists of government and state-owned enterprises (SOEs). Government intervenes in the economy to correct market failures and promote social welfare.\n\n### Reasons for Government Intervention\n\n1. **Provide public goods**: Defence, street lights, roads — the market will not provide these because people can benefit without paying (free-rider problem)\n2. **Correct externalities**: Pollution (negative externality) is overproduced; education (positive externality) is underprovided\n3. **Reduce inequality**: Markets create unequal outcomes; government redistributes through taxes and grants\n4. **Stabilise the economy**: Counter business cycle fluctuations through fiscal and monetary policy\n5. **Regulate monopolies**: Prevent abuse of market power (Competition Commission)\n6. **Protect consumers**: Ensure safety, fair pricing, and accurate information\n7. **Provide merit goods**: Education, healthcare, housing — beneficial to society but underprovided by markets\n\n### Methods of Involvement\n\nGovernment uses several tools to intervene in the economy:\n- Taxation (direct and indirect taxes)\n- Government spending\n- Subsidies\n- Price controls (maximum and minimum prices)\n- Legislation and regulation\n- State-owned enterprises"),
  t(2, "### Indirect Taxes\n\nIndirect taxes are taxes on goods and services (not on income directly). The producer pays the tax to SARS but passes some or all of it on to consumers through higher prices.\n\n**Types of indirect taxes in SA:**\n\n| Tax | Rate | What is taxed |\n|-----|------|---------------|\n| **Value-Added Tax (VAT)** | 15% | Most goods and services |\n| **Excise duties** | Varies | Alcohol, tobacco, fuel (\"sin taxes\") |\n| **Customs duties** | Varies | Imported goods |\n| **Fuel levy** | Per litre | Petrol and diesel |\n| **Sugar tax** | Per gram | Sugary beverages |\n\n**Effect of indirect taxes on supply and demand:**\n- Tax increases the cost of production for suppliers\n- Supply curve shifts to the left (upward by the amount of the tax)\n- Equilibrium price rises and equilibrium quantity falls\n- The burden of the tax is shared between consumers (higher price) and producers (lower revenue after tax)\n\n**VAT-exempt items in SA:**\nBasic foodstuffs are zero-rated (0% VAT): brown bread, maize meal, rice, milk, eggs, vegetable oil, tinned pilchards, lentils, dried beans. This protects poor households."),
  q(3, "VAT in South Africa is currently set at:",
    ["10%", "14%", "15%", "20%"], 2,
    "VAT in South Africa is 15%. It was increased from 14% to 15% in April 2018. Certain basic foodstuffs are zero-rated to protect low-income households."),
  t(4, "### Subsidies\n\nA **subsidy** is a payment by government to producers to reduce their costs of production and make goods more affordable.\n\n**Effect of subsidies on supply and demand:**\n- Subsidy reduces the cost of production for suppliers\n- Supply curve shifts to the right (downward by the amount of the subsidy)\n- Equilibrium price falls and equilibrium quantity rises\n- Consumers benefit from lower prices; producers may benefit from higher sales\n\n**Examples of subsidies in SA:**\n- Housing subsidies (RDP/BNG houses for low-income families)\n- Agricultural subsidies (support for emerging farmers)\n- Education subsidies (no-fee schools, NSFAS bursaries)\n- Public transport subsidies (PRASA rail, bus services)\n- Renewable energy subsidies (Renewable Energy Independent Power Producer Programme)\n\n**Arguments for subsidies:**\n- Make essential goods and services affordable\n- Support emerging industries and small businesses\n- Create jobs and reduce poverty\n\n**Arguments against subsidies:**\n- Expensive for the government (opportunity cost)\n- May create dependency\n- Can be inefficient if poorly targeted\n- May distort market prices"),
  fb(5, "An indirect tax shifts the supply curve to the ___, while a subsidy shifts the supply curve to the ___.",
    ["left", "right"],
    "Taxes increase production costs (supply decreases/shifts left). Subsidies reduce production costs (supply increases/shifts right). Both change the equilibrium price and quantity."),
  t(6, "### Welfare and Social Protection\n\nThe South African government provides a social safety net to protect vulnerable groups:\n\n**Social grants (SASSA):**\n\n| Grant | Amount (approximate) | Recipients |\n|-------|---------------------|------------|\n| **Old Age Grant** | R2 180/month | Citizens 60 years and older |\n| **Child Support Grant** | R530/month per child | Primary caregivers of children under 18 |\n| **Disability Grant** | R2 180/month | People with disabilities |\n| **Foster Care Grant** | R1 180/month | Foster parents |\n| **Care Dependency Grant** | R2 180/month | Caregivers of disabled children |\n| **War Veterans Grant** | R2 200/month | Military veterans |\n| **Social Relief of Distress** | R370/month | Adults in temporary need |\n\nOver **18 million** South Africans receive social grants, making it one of the largest social protection programmes in the developing world.\n\n**Other welfare measures:**\n- Free basic water (6 kilolitres per month per household)\n- Free basic electricity (50 kWh per month)\n- No-fee schools\n- Free primary healthcare at government clinics\n- RDP/BNG housing for low-income families"),
  q(7, "Approximately how many South Africans receive social grants from SASSA?",
    ["5 million", "10 million", "18 million", "40 million"], 2,
    "Over 18 million South Africans receive social grants, which is roughly one-third of the population. The child support grant has the most beneficiaries, followed by the old age grant."),
  t(8, "### Maximum and Minimum Prices\n\n**Maximum price (price ceiling):**\nA price set by government **below** the equilibrium price. It aims to make goods affordable for consumers.\n\n- Effect: Creates a **shortage** (quantity demanded > quantity supplied)\n- Producers supply less at the lower price\n- Example: Rent controls (limit how much landlords can charge for housing)\n\n**Minimum price (price floor):**\nA price set by government **above** the equilibrium price. It aims to protect producers or workers.\n\n- Effect: Creates a **surplus** (quantity supplied > quantity demanded)\n- Consumers demand less at the higher price\n- Example: **National minimum wage** (R27.58/hour in 2024) — protects workers from being paid too little\n\n**SA minimum wage:**\n- Protects low-wage workers from exploitation\n- Critics argue it may reduce employment (firms hire fewer workers at higher wages)\n- Supporters argue it reduces poverty and increases consumer spending\n- Different rates apply to domestic workers, farm workers, and EPWP workers\n\n**Production:**\nGovernment also intervenes in production through:\n- SOEs (Eskom, Transnet, SAA, SABC)\n- Regulation of industries (telecommunications, banking, mining)\n- Competition policy (preventing monopolies and cartels)"),
  fb(9, "A maximum price is set ___ equilibrium and creates a shortage. A minimum price is set ___ equilibrium and creates a surplus.",
    ["below", "above"],
    "Price ceilings (maximum prices) keep prices low for consumers but cause shortages. Price floors (minimum prices) protect producers/workers but cause surpluses."),
  q(10, "The national minimum wage in South Africa is an example of:",
    ["A maximum price", "A minimum price (price floor)", "A subsidy", "An indirect tax"], 1,
    "The minimum wage is a price floor set above the equilibrium wage in some labour markets. It prevents employers from paying workers less than R27.58 per hour, protecting low-wage workers."),
];

// =============================================================================
// CHAPTER 6: Production Possibility Curve (Term 2, Weeks 7-9)
// =============================================================================

blockNum = 0;
const ch6_lesson1 = [
  t(1, "## The Production Possibility Curve (PPC)\n\nThe **Production Possibility Curve** (also called the Production Possibility Frontier — PPF) shows the maximum possible combinations of two goods that an economy can produce when all resources are used efficiently.\n\n### Assumptions of the PPC\n\n1. Only **two goods** are produced\n2. The quantity and quality of resources are **fixed** (in the short run)\n3. **Technology is constant** (no improvements)\n4. All resources are **fully and efficiently employed**\n\n### Reading the PPC\n\n- Any point **on** the PPC: Resources are fully and efficiently used (productively efficient)\n- Any point **inside** the PPC: Resources are underutilised or used inefficiently (unemployment, waste)\n- Any point **outside** the PPC: Currently unattainable with available resources and technology\n\n**Example:** An economy produces food and clothing.\n\n| Combination | Food (million tonnes) | Clothing (million units) |\n|-------------|----------------------|------------------------|\n| A | 0 | 10 |\n| B | 2 | 9 |\n| C | 4 | 7 |\n| D | 6 | 4 |\n| E | 8 | 0 |"),
  t(2, "### Opportunity Cost on the PPC\n\nThe PPC demonstrates opportunity cost graphically. Moving from one point to another on the curve means producing more of one good and less of the other.\n\n**Example:**\nMoving from B to C: Food increases by 2 million tonnes, but clothing decreases by 2 million units.\nOpportunity cost of 2 million extra tonnes of food = 2 million units of clothing.\n\nMoving from C to D: Food increases by 2 million tonnes, but clothing decreases by 3 million units.\nOpportunity cost of 2 million extra tonnes of food = 3 million units of clothing.\n\n**The opportunity cost increases** as you produce more of one good. This is because resources are not equally suited to producing both goods. As you divert more resources from clothing to food, you use resources less suited to food production, making each additional unit more costly.\n\nThis is called the **law of increasing opportunity cost**, and it gives the PPC its **concave (bowed-out) shape**.\n\nIf resources were perfectly substitutable, the PPC would be a straight line with constant opportunity cost."),
  q(3, "A point inside the Production Possibility Curve represents:",
    ["Maximum efficiency", "Full employment of resources", "Inefficiency or unemployment of resources", "An unattainable level of production"], 2,
    "A point inside the PPC means the economy is not using all its resources or is using them inefficiently. This could be due to unemployment, underemployment, or waste. The economy could produce more of both goods by moving toward the PPC."),
  t(4, "### Shifts of the PPC\n\nThe PPC can shift outward or inward, indicating changes in the economy's productive capacity.\n\n**Outward shift (economic growth):**\nThe economy can now produce more of both goods. Causes include:\n- Discovery of new natural resources (e.g., new mineral deposits)\n- Population growth (more workers available)\n- Improved technology (more efficient production)\n- Increased investment in capital goods (new factories, machines)\n- Better education and training (improved human capital)\n- Immigration of skilled workers\n\n**Inward shift (economic decline):**\nThe economy's productive capacity decreases. Causes include:\n- Natural disasters (droughts, floods, earthquakes)\n- Wars or political instability\n- Epidemics (HIV/AIDS reduced SA's labour force)\n- Emigration of skilled workers (brain drain)\n- Depletion of natural resources\n- Infrastructure deterioration\n\n**Uneven shift:**\nIf technology improves in only one industry, the PPC shifts outward on that axis only, changing its shape."),
  fb(5, "An outward shift of the PPC indicates economic ___. A point outside the PPC is currently ___.",
    ["growth", "unattainable"],
    "Economic growth expands the PPC outward, meaning the economy can produce more. Points outside the current PPC require more resources or better technology to achieve."),
  t(6, "### The PPC and Scarcity, Choice, and Opportunity Cost\n\nThe PPC beautifully illustrates the three core economic concepts:\n\n**Scarcity:** The PPC exists because resources are limited. If resources were unlimited, we could produce infinite amounts of everything (no PPC needed).\n\n**Choice:** Society must choose which combination of goods to produce (which point on the PPC).\n\n**Opportunity cost:** Moving along the PPC means giving up some of one good to get more of another.\n\n### The PPC and Economic Efficiency\n\n**Productive efficiency:** Achieved at any point ON the PPC — all resources are fully utilised.\n\n**Allocative efficiency:** Achieved at the specific point on the PPC that produces the combination of goods most desired by society.\n\n**SA context:**\nSouth Africa operates inside its PPC — we are not using all resources efficiently. Key reasons:\n- High unemployment (over 32% of the labour force is idle)\n- Load shedding causes factories to stop production\n- Crime and corruption waste resources\n- Skills mismatch means available workers cannot fill available jobs\n\nIf SA could move to its PPC by employing all unemployed workers and fixing electricity supply, output would increase significantly without needing new resources."),
  q(7, "Which of the following would cause an outward shift of the PPC?",
    ["An increase in unemployment", "A natural disaster destroying infrastructure", "Investment in new technology and machinery", "Emigration of skilled workers"], 2,
    "New technology and machinery increase the economy's productive capacity, shifting the PPC outward. The other options all reduce productive capacity (inward shift or movement toward a point inside the PPC)."),
  t(8, "### Consumer Goods vs Capital Goods\n\nSociety faces a fundamental choice on the PPC between producing **consumer goods** (for immediate satisfaction) and **capital goods** (for future production).\n\n**Consumer goods:** Food, clothing, entertainment — satisfy current wants\n**Capital goods:** Machines, factories, infrastructure — increase future production capacity\n\n**The trade-off:**\n- A country that produces mostly consumer goods enjoys higher current living standards but grows slowly (PPC shifts out slowly)\n- A country that produces mostly capital goods sacrifices current consumption but enjoys faster future growth (PPC shifts out quickly)\n\n**Examples:**\n- **China** invested heavily in capital goods (infrastructure, factories) over the past 40 years, sacrificing current consumption but achieving rapid economic growth\n- **SA** needs to invest more in capital goods (infrastructure, renewable energy) to shift its PPC outward and grow faster\n\n**The NDP recommends** that SA increase investment to 30% of GDP (from approximately 14-16%) to achieve the growth needed to reduce unemployment and poverty.\n\nThis is a central challenge for developing countries: how to balance immediate needs (feeding, housing, and educating the population) with long-term investment (building the factories and infrastructure needed for future growth)."),
  fb(9, "The PPC demonstrates scarcity, ___, and opportunity cost. A concave PPC reflects the law of ___ opportunity cost.",
    ["choice", "increasing"],
    "The PPC shows that scarcity forces choices, and every choice has an opportunity cost. The concave shape shows that opportunity cost increases as production of one good increases, because resources are not perfectly adaptable."),
  q(10, "If South Africa reduces unemployment and moves from a point inside the PPC to a point on the PPC, this represents:",
    ["Economic growth", "An outward shift of the PPC", "More efficient use of existing resources", "A decrease in opportunity cost"], 2,
    "Moving from inside the PPC to a point on the PPC means using existing resources more fully and efficiently, not an increase in total resources. An outward shift of the PPC would require more resources or better technology."),
];

