// CHAPTER 7: Economic Growth, Development, and Globalisation (Term 3, Weeks 1-3)
// =============================================================================

blockNum = 0;
const ch7_lesson1 = [
  t(1, "## Economic Growth and Development\n\n### Historical Overview\n\nEconomic development has evolved through distinct stages:\n\n**Self-sufficiency era:**\n- Communities produced everything they needed themselves\n- Subsistence agriculture was the dominant activity\n- No trade or specialisation\n\n**Barter and trade:**\n- Communities began exchanging surplus goods\n- Specialisation developed (some hunted, some farmed, some made tools)\n- Barter required a **double coincidence of wants** (you have what I want AND I have what you want)\n\n**Emergence of money:**\n- Money solved the problems of barter\n- Cattle, shells, beads, and eventually coins and notes were used as money\n- Enabled specialisation, trade, and economic growth\n\n**Evolution of markets:**\n- Surplus production led to trade and markets\n- Mercantile law (commercial law) developed to regulate trade\n- The development of business forms: sole traders, partnerships, companies\n- Financial institutions emerged (banks, stock exchanges)"),
  t(2, "### Industrialisation and Modern Development\n\n**The Industrial Revolution (late 1700s):**\n- Started in Britain with mechanisation (steam engine, spinning jenny)\n- Factories replaced cottage industries\n- Urbanisation accelerated as workers moved to cities\n- Massive increase in production and economic growth\n\n**Stages of development:**\n\n| Stage | Features | SA Example |\n|-------|----------|------------|\n| **Agricultural** | Economy based on farming, low technology | Pre-colonial SA |\n| **Industrial** | Manufacturing becomes dominant, urbanisation | SA mining boom (1880s onwards) |\n| **Post-industrial** | Services dominate, knowledge economy | Modern SA (~65% services) |\n\n**Government's role in development:**\n- Regulation of markets and trade\n- Taxation and mobilisation of finance\n- Investment in infrastructure (roads, railways, ports, electricity)\n- Industrial policy to support manufacturing\n- SA used both protectionism (during apartheid) and trade liberalisation (post-1994)\n\n**Technological progress:**\n- Mechanisation of production\n- Development of energy sources (coal, electricity, renewable energy)\n- Digital revolution (computers, internet, mobile technology)\n- Automation and artificial intelligence (emerging challenge for jobs)"),
  q(3, "The double coincidence of wants is a problem associated with:",
    ["The money economy", "The barter system", "Modern banking", "International trade"], 1,
    "Barter requires both parties to want what the other has to offer at the same time. This 'double coincidence of wants' made trade difficult and was solved by the invention of money as a medium of exchange."),
  t(4, "### Saving and Investment\n\nSaving and investment are crucial for economic growth.\n\n**Saving:** Setting aside current income for future use\n- **Household saving**: Money deposited in banks, retirement funds, unit trusts\n- **Government saving**: Budget surplus (revenue exceeds spending)\n- **Corporate saving**: Retained profits not distributed as dividends\n\nSA has a very low household savings rate (below 1% of GDP), which limits the funds available for investment.\n\n**Investment:** Using saved funds to create new capital goods or acquire financial assets\n- **Fixed investment**: Buildings, machinery, infrastructure\n- **Portfolio investment**: Buying shares, bonds, and other financial instruments\n- **Foreign Direct Investment (FDI)**: Foreign companies establishing businesses in SA\n\n**The link between saving and investment:**\n- Banks take deposits (savings) from households\n- Banks lend these funds to businesses for investment\n- Investment creates new productive capacity\n- More production leads to more income and more saving\n- This creates a virtuous cycle of growth\n\n**SA challenge:** Low savings means less domestic capital available for investment, increasing reliance on foreign capital (which can be volatile)."),
  fb(5, "The ___ system required a double coincidence of wants. SA has a very ___ household savings rate, limiting funds available for investment.",
    ["barter", "low"],
    "The barter system's limitations led to the development of money. SA's low savings rate constrains investment and economic growth."),
  t(6, "### Globalisation — An Introduction\n\n**Globalisation** is the increasing interconnection and interdependence of economies worldwide through trade, investment, technology, and the movement of people.\n\n**How globalisation affects SA:**\n\n**Positive effects:**\n- Access to global markets for SA exports (minerals, fruit, wine, vehicles)\n- Foreign investment brings capital, technology, and skills\n- Consumers have access to a wider variety of products\n- Technology transfer (access to global innovation)\n- Tourism creates jobs and foreign exchange\n\n**Negative effects:**\n- Competition from cheap imports hurts local industries (clothing, textiles)\n- Capital flight — investors can move money out quickly, weakening the rand\n- Brain drain — skilled South Africans emigrate to developed countries\n- Cultural homogenisation — dominance of Western brands and media\n- Exposure to global economic shocks (2008 GFC, COVID-19)\n\n**SA's global connections:**\n- Member of BRICS (Brazil, Russia, India, China, South Africa)\n- Member of the African Union (AU) and SADC\n- Participant in AfCFTA (African Continental Free Trade Area)\n- Beneficiary of AGOA (preferential access to US markets)"),
  q(7, "Which of the following is a negative effect of globalisation for South Africa?",
    ["Access to foreign investment", "Increased variety of consumer goods", "Competition from cheap imports harming local industry", "Technology transfer from developed countries"], 2,
    "Cheap imports, especially from China, have harmed SA's clothing, textiles, and manufacturing industries. Local producers cannot compete on price, leading to factory closures and job losses."),
  t(8, "### Economic Growth vs Economic Development\n\n**Economic growth** is the increase in real GDP over time. It is a **quantitative** measure — how much more the economy produces.\n\n**Economic development** is a broader concept that includes improvements in the **quality of life**, living standards, health, education, and freedom. It is a **qualitative** measure.\n\n| Economic Growth | Economic Development |\n|----------------|---------------------|\n| Increase in real GDP | Improvement in quality of life |\n| Quantitative | Qualitative |\n| Measured by GDP growth rate | Measured by HDI and other indicators |\n| Can occur without development | Requires growth AND better distribution |\n\n**A country can have growth without development:**\nIf GDP grows but only the wealthy benefit, the majority remain poor. This describes much of SA's experience.\n\n**The Human Development Index (HDI):**\nCombines three dimensions:\n1. **Health**: Life expectancy at birth\n2. **Education**: Mean and expected years of schooling\n3. **Income**: GNI per capita\n\nSA HDI: approximately 0.713 (ranked around 109 globally). Despite being an upper-middle-income country, SA's HDI is dragged down by low life expectancy (HIV/AIDS), poor education quality, and extreme inequality."),
  fb(9, "Economic growth is a ___ measure, while economic development is a ___ measure that includes quality of life improvements.",
    ["quantitative", "qualitative"],
    "Growth only measures more output. Development measures whether people's lives are actually improving. A country needs both growth AND fair distribution for true development."),
  q(10, "The HDI combines which three dimensions?",
    ["Income, exports, and inflation", "Health, education, and income", "GDP, unemployment, and inflation", "Technology, trade, and investment"], 1,
    "The Human Development Index measures health (life expectancy), education (years of schooling), and income (GNI per capita). It provides a broader measure of development than GDP alone."),
];

