blockNum = 0;
const ch8_lesson1 = [
  t(1, "## Population\n\n### Population Size and Growth\n\nSouth Africa's population is approximately **62 million** people (Statistics SA estimates).\n\n**Population growth rate** = ((Births - Deaths + Net migration) / Population) x 100\n\n**Natural growth rate** = Birth rate - Death rate (excluding migration)\n\n**Key population terms:**\n- **Birth rate (fertility rate)**: Number of live births per 1 000 people per year\n- **Death rate (mortality rate)**: Number of deaths per 1 000 people per year\n- **Net migration**: Immigration minus emigration\n- **Infant mortality rate**: Deaths of babies under 1 year per 1 000 live births\n- **Life expectancy**: Average number of years a person is expected to live\n\n**SA population facts:**\n- Population growth rate: approximately 1.2% per year\n- Life expectancy: approximately 65 years (lower than developed countries due to HIV/AIDS)\n- High proportion of young people (the \"youth bulge\")\n- Net immigration from other African countries\n- Significant urbanisation (over 65% of the population lives in urban areas)"),
  t(2, "### The Demographic Cycle\n\nThe **demographic transition** describes how population growth changes as a country develops:\n\n| Stage | Birth Rate | Death Rate | Population Growth | Example |\n|-------|-----------|------------|-------------------|--------|\n| **Stage 1: Pre-transition** | High | High | Low/stable | Pre-colonial societies |\n| **Stage 2: Early transition** | High | Falling | Rapid growth | Many African countries |\n| **Stage 3: Late transition** | Falling | Low | Slowing growth | South Africa, Brazil |\n| **Stage 4: Post-transition** | Low | Low | Low/stable or declining | Japan, Germany |\n\n**SA is in Stage 3:**\n- Birth rates are declining (education of women, access to contraception, urbanisation)\n- Death rates have decreased (improved healthcare, antiretrovirals for HIV)\n- Population is still growing, but the rate is slowing\n\n**Factors affecting SA population:**\n- HIV/AIDS (still the leading cause of death; ARV treatment has improved life expectancy)\n- Migration (immigration from Zimbabwe, Mozambique, and other countries)\n- Urbanisation (people moving from rural to urban areas for work)\n- Government family planning programmes\n- Poverty and inequality affect health outcomes"),
  q(3, "South Africa is in which stage of the demographic transition?",
    ["Stage 1: Pre-transition", "Stage 2: Early transition", "Stage 3: Late transition", "Stage 4: Post-transition"], 2,
    "SA is in Stage 3 (Late transition) with declining birth rates (due to education, urbanisation, and contraception) and low but stable death rates (improved healthcare). Population is still growing but the growth rate is slowing."),
  t(4, "### Population Structure — Age Distribution\n\nThe age structure of a population is shown using a **population pyramid** — a graph showing the distribution of age groups and gender.\n\n**SA population structure:**\n- Broad base (many young people under 30 — the \"youth bulge\")\n- Narrower middle (working-age adults)\n- Narrow top (fewer elderly people due to lower life expectancy)\n\n**Dependency ratio** = (People under 15 + People over 65) / Working-age population (15-64) x 100\n\n- A high dependency ratio means more people depend on the working population for support\n- SA has a relatively high dependency ratio\n\n**Economic implications of SA's age structure:**\n\n| Age group | Challenge | Policy response |\n|-----------|----------|------------------|\n| Youth (0-14) | Need education, healthcare, nutrition | Investment in schools, child support grants |\n| Working age (15-64) | Need jobs — unemployment above 32% | Job creation, skills development |\n| Elderly (65+) | Need pensions and healthcare | Old age grants, geriatric care |\n\n**The youth bulge** can be a **demographic dividend** (boost to growth) if young people are employed and productive, or a **demographic disaster** if they remain unemployed and frustrated."),
  fb(5, "SA has approximately ___ million people. The dependency ratio measures the number of dependent people relative to the ___-age population.",
    ["62", "working"],
    "SA's large young population could drive growth if educated and employed, but currently most face unemployment, making the youth bulge a demographic challenge rather than a dividend."),
  t(6, "### The South African Labour Force\n\nThe **labour force** consists of all people aged 15-64 who are employed or actively seeking employment.\n\n**Key labour market concepts:**\n- **Economically Active Population (EAP)**: People who are employed or unemployed but looking for work\n- **Employed**: People currently working (formal or informal sector)\n- **Unemployed**: People without a job who are actively looking for work\n- **Not economically active**: People not working and not seeking work (students, homemakers, retired, discouraged workers)\n\n**SA labour force statistics (approximate):**\n- Working-age population: ~40 million\n- Labour force (EAP): ~24 million\n- Employed: ~16 million\n- Unemployed (narrow): ~8 million\n- Not economically active: ~16 million (includes discouraged workers)\n\n**Unemployment rate:**\n- **Narrow (official)**: Unemployed / EAP x 100 = approximately 32%\n- **Expanded (broad)**: Includes discouraged workers = approximately 42%\n\nSA has one of the highest unemployment rates in the world, with youth unemployment (15-24) exceeding 60%."),
  q(7, "The expanded definition of unemployment includes:",
    ["Only people who have registered with the Department of Labour", "People who are unemployed and actively looking for work", "Discouraged workers who have stopped looking for jobs", "People who are employed in the informal sector"], 2,
    "The expanded definition includes discouraged workers — people who want to work but have given up looking because they believe no work is available. This gives a more accurate picture of joblessness in SA (approximately 42%)."),
  t(8, "### Geographic Distribution and Migration\n\n**Geographic distribution of SA's population:**\n- **Gauteng**: Most populated province (~16 million, ~26% of SA population)\n- **KwaZulu-Natal**: Second most populated (~12 million)\n- **Urban areas**: Over 65% of the population\n- **Rural areas**: Mainly in former homelands (Eastern Cape, Limpopo, KwaZulu-Natal)\n\n**Internal migration:**\n- Urbanisation continues as people move from rural to urban areas seeking employment\n- Gauteng is the main destination (economic hub)\n- Creates pressure on housing, services, and infrastructure in cities\n- Rural areas lose productive workers (young, skilled)\n\n**International migration:**\n- SA receives significant immigration from other African countries (Zimbabwe, Mozambique, Nigeria, Democratic Republic of Congo)\n- Immigrants contribute to the economy as workers, entrepreneurs, and consumers\n- Xenophobia and anti-immigrant violence remain serious concerns\n- Brain drain: Skilled South Africans emigrate to UK, Australia, Canada, and USA\n\n**Projected population growth:**\n- SA population expected to reach approximately 70 million by 2040\n- Growth rate declining due to lower birth rates and HIV/AIDS\n- Urbanisation will continue, putting further pressure on cities"),
  fb(9, "South Africa's narrow unemployment rate is approximately ___%. ___ is the most populated province.",
    ["32", "Gauteng"],
    "SA's 32% narrow unemployment rate is one of the highest globally. Gauteng, home to Johannesburg and Pretoria, is the economic hub and most populated province."),
  q(10, "The 'demographic dividend' occurs when:",
    ["A country's birth rate exceeds its death rate", "A large working-age population is employed and productive", "There are more elderly people than young people", "Immigration exceeds emigration"], 1,
    "The demographic dividend is the economic boost that comes when a large young working-age population is employed productively, generating growth and tax revenue. SA has the potential for this but currently wastes it through high youth unemployment."),
];

