blockNum = 0;
const ch9_lesson1 = [
  t(1, "## Labour Relations in South Africa\n\nLabour relations (also called industrial relations) describe the relationship between employers, employees, and the government in the workplace.\n\n### The Labour Market\n\nThe labour market is where the demand for labour (by employers) and the supply of labour (by workers) interact to determine wages and employment levels.\n\n**Demand for labour:**\n- Firms demand labour to produce goods and services\n- Demand for labour is a **derived demand** — it depends on the demand for the goods and services workers produce\n- Higher wages (ceteris paribus) lead to lower demand for labour (inverse relationship)\n\n**Supply of labour:**\n- Workers supply their labour in exchange for wages\n- Higher wages attract more workers (direct relationship)\n- Supply depends on: population size, skills, education, working conditions, and wages in other jobs\n\n**Labour market equilibrium:**\n- Where labour demand meets labour supply\n- Determines the equilibrium wage rate and level of employment\n- In SA, the equilibrium is disrupted by minimum wages, union bargaining, skills shortages, and structural unemployment"),
  t(2, "### Key Role Players in Labour Relations\n\n**Employees (workers):**\n- Supply labour in exchange for wages\n- Have rights under the Constitution and labour legislation\n- Join trade unions to strengthen their bargaining power\n\n**Employers:**\n- Demand labour to produce goods and services\n- Have the right to manage and direct the workplace\n- Form employer organisations to represent their interests\n\n**Trade unions:**\n- Organisations of workers that negotiate collectively with employers\n- Protect workers' rights and interests\n- Major SA federations: COSATU, SAFTU, FEDUSA, NACTU\n- Rights: to strike, organise, and bargain collectively\n\n**Government:**\n- Makes labour laws and policies\n- Mediates disputes through CCMA\n- Enforces labour standards (minimum wages, working conditions)\n- Key government department: Department of Employment and Labour\n\n**The tripartite relationship:**\nLabour relations involve three parties (tripartism):\n1. Workers (represented by trade unions)\n2. Employers (represented by employer organisations)\n3. Government (as regulator and mediator)"),
  q(3, "Demand for labour is called a 'derived demand' because:",
    ["Workers derive satisfaction from working", "It depends on the demand for the goods workers produce", "Employers derive profits from labour", "It is determined by government policy"], 1,
    "Demand for labour is 'derived' from the demand for the final product. If demand for cars increases, the demand for car factory workers also increases. If demand for cars falls, fewer workers are needed."),
  t(4, "### Labour Legislation in South Africa\n\nSA has comprehensive labour laws to protect workers and regulate the workplace:\n\n**Labour Relations Act (LRA), 1995:**\n- Governs the relationship between employers, employees, and unions\n- Protects the right to form and join trade unions\n- Establishes collective bargaining procedures\n- Regulates strikes and lockouts\n- Created the CCMA (Commission for Conciliation, Mediation, and Arbitration)\n\n**Basic Conditions of Employment Act (BCEA), 1997:**\n- Sets minimum standards for working conditions\n- Maximum working hours (45 hours per week)\n- Overtime pay (1.5 times normal rate)\n- Annual leave (21 consecutive days per year)\n- Sick leave, maternity leave, family responsibility leave\n\n**Employment Equity Act (EEA), 1998:**\n- Promotes equal opportunity and fair treatment\n- Eliminates unfair discrimination\n- Requires affirmative action for designated groups (Black people, women, people with disabilities)\n\n**Skills Development Act, 1998:**\n- Promotes skills development through SETAs (Sector Education and Training Authorities)\n- Funded by the skills development levy (1% of payroll)"),
  fb(5, "The ___ Act governs the relationship between employers, employees, and trade unions. The CCMA stands for Commission for Conciliation, ___, and Arbitration.",
    ["Labour Relations", "Mediation"],
    "The LRA (1995) is the cornerstone of SA labour law. The CCMA resolves workplace disputes through conciliation, mediation, and arbitration rather than costly court proceedings."),
  t(6, "### Collective Bargaining\n\n**Collective bargaining** is the process where trade unions negotiate with employers on behalf of workers to reach agreements on wages, working conditions, and other employment matters.\n\n**Levels of collective bargaining:**\n\n| Level | Description |\n|-------|-------------|\n| **Plant level** | Negotiations at a single workplace (one factory, one office) |\n| **Sectoral level** | Negotiations for an entire sector through Bargaining Councils (e.g., metal and engineering sector) |\n| **National level** | NEDLAC (National Economic Development and Labour Council) — government, labour, business, and community discuss national policy |\n\n**Bargaining Councils:**\n- Joint bodies of employer organisations and trade unions for a specific sector\n- Negotiate wages and conditions for the entire sector\n- Agreements can be extended to non-parties (all employers in the sector must comply)\n\n**NEDLAC:**\n- Self-governing body where government, organised labour, organised business, and community organisations consult on economic policy\n- Considers proposed labour legislation before it goes to Parliament\n- Four chambers: Public Finance, Trade and Industry, Labour Market, Development"),
  q(7, "NEDLAC brings together which groups to discuss economic policy?",
    ["Only employers and workers", "Government, labour, business, and community", "Only government and trade unions", "Only businesses and consumers"], 1,
    "NEDLAC (National Economic Development and Labour Council) is a tripartite-plus body where government, organised labour, organised business, and community organisations consult on labour, trade, and economic policy."),
  t(8, "### Dispute Resolution\n\nWhen employers and employees cannot agree, disputes must be resolved:\n\n**Step 1 — Conciliation (CCMA):**\n- A neutral commissioner helps both parties talk through the dispute\n- Aim: reach an agreement without further conflict\n- Most disputes are resolved at this stage\n\n**Step 2 — Mediation:**\n- A mediator actively suggests solutions\n- Non-binding — parties can still reject the proposal\n\n**Step 3 — Arbitration:**\n- An arbitrator hears both sides and makes a binding decision\n- Like a private court — the decision must be accepted\n\n**Step 4 — Labour Court:**\n- If arbitration fails or involves complex legal issues\n- The Labour Court has the authority of the High Court\n- Appeal can go to the Labour Appeal Court\n\n**Strikes and lockouts:**\n- **Strike**: Workers collectively refuse to work to pressure the employer (protected if proper procedures are followed)\n- **Lockout**: Employer prevents workers from entering the workplace\n- Both are legal if correct procedures (notice, conciliation attempt) are followed\n- SA has a history of significant strikes, including the 2012 Marikana tragedy"),
  fb(9, "The three steps of dispute resolution are conciliation, mediation, and ___. A ___ is when workers collectively refuse to work.",
    ["arbitration", "strike"],
    "The CCMA handles most workplace disputes through conciliation, mediation, and arbitration. Strikes are a last resort for workers and must follow proper legal procedures to be protected."),
  q(10, "Which act sets the minimum working conditions in South Africa, including maximum working hours and leave entitlements?",
    ["The Labour Relations Act", "The Basic Conditions of Employment Act", "The Employment Equity Act", "The Skills Development Act"], 1,
    "The BCEA (1997) sets minimum standards for working hours (45 per week), overtime pay (1.5x), annual leave (21 days), sick leave, and maternity leave. It establishes a floor below which no employer can go."),
];

