blockNum = 0;
const ch10_lesson1 = [
  t(1, "## Unemployment in South Africa\n\n**Unemployment** exists when people who are willing and able to work cannot find employment.\n\n### Measuring Unemployment\n\n**Narrow (official) definition:**\nA person is unemployed if they:\n1. Did not work during the reference period\n2. Were available to work\n3. Took active steps to find work (sent CVs, attended interviews)\n\n**Expanded (broad) definition:**\nIncludes the above plus **discouraged work-seekers** — people who want work but have given up looking.\n\n**SA unemployment statistics:**\n- Narrow unemployment rate: approximately 32%\n- Expanded unemployment rate: approximately 42%\n- Youth unemployment (15-24): over 60%\n- Total unemployed: approximately 8 million (narrow) or 12 million (expanded)\n\n**Unemployment rate** = (Number unemployed / Labour force) x 100\n\nSouth Africa has one of the highest unemployment rates in the world, and the problem is particularly severe among young people and in rural areas."),
  t(2, "### Characteristics of SA Unemployment\n\nUnemployment in South Africa has distinct features that make it especially challenging:\n\n**Racial dimension:**\n- Black African unemployment is much higher than other racial groups\n- Legacy of apartheid education and economic exclusion\n- BBBEE aims to address this but progress is slow\n\n**Gender dimension:**\n- Women have higher unemployment rates than men\n- Women also face lower wages and fewer opportunities in some sectors\n\n**Age dimension:**\n- Youth unemployment (15-34) exceeds 45%\n- Young people lack experience, making it harder to find first jobs\n- The \"Not in Employment, Education, or Training\" (NEET) group is approximately 3.5 million\n\n**Geographic dimension:**\n- Rural areas have higher unemployment than urban areas\n- Former homelands (Eastern Cape, Limpopo) have the worst rates\n- Gauteng has the highest employment but also large numbers of unemployed\n\n**Education dimension:**\n- Those without matric have much higher unemployment rates\n- However, even graduates face high unemployment (skills mismatch)\n- Technical and vocational skills are in high demand but undersupplied"),
  q(3, "South Africa's expanded unemployment rate is approximately:",
    ["15%", "25%", "32%", "42%"], 3,
    "The expanded unemployment rate (~42%) includes discouraged workers who have given up looking for work. The narrow (official) rate (~32%) only counts those actively seeking employment."),
  t(4, "### Causes of Unemployment\n\nUnemployment in SA has multiple, interconnected causes:\n\n**1. Structural unemployment:**\n- Mismatch between the skills workers have and the skills employers need\n- SA education system produces many graduates with skills the economy does not require\n- Technological change eliminates some jobs (automation, digitisation)\n- Decline of labour-intensive sectors (mining, agriculture)\n\n**2. Cyclical unemployment:**\n- Caused by downswings in the business cycle\n- During recessions, demand falls and firms retrench workers\n- SA experienced major job losses during COVID-19 (2020)\n\n**3. Frictional unemployment:**\n- Temporary unemployment as people move between jobs\n- Normal in any economy\n- Usually short-term\n\n**4. Seasonal unemployment:**\n- Industries that depend on seasons (agriculture, tourism, retail)\n- Workers hired for peak periods and laid off in quiet times\n\n**5. Institutional unemployment:**\n- Caused by labour market regulations\n- Minimum wages above equilibrium may reduce demand for labour\n- Strict labour laws may discourage hiring\n- Trade union wage demands may price some workers out of the market"),
  fb(5, "Structural unemployment is caused by a ___ between workers' skills and employers' needs. Cyclical unemployment is caused by ___ in the business cycle.",
    ["mismatch", "downswings"],
    "Structural unemployment is the most serious type in SA because it requires education reform, not just economic recovery. Cyclical unemployment recovers when the economy grows."),
  t(6, "### Consequences of Unemployment\n\nUnemployment has devastating effects on individuals, households, and the economy:\n\n**Effects on individuals and households:**\n- Loss of income and financial hardship\n- Poverty and hunger\n- Loss of self-esteem and dignity\n- Depression, anxiety, and mental health problems\n- Substance abuse\n- Family breakdowns and domestic violence\n- Social exclusion and marginalisation\n\n**Effects on the economy:**\n- Lower GDP (lost production from idle workers)\n- Higher government spending on grants and welfare\n- Lower tax revenue (fewer taxpayers)\n- Increased inequality\n- Crime and social instability\n- Brain drain (skilled people emigrate)\n\n**Effects on society:**\n- Increased crime rates\n- Social unrest and service delivery protests\n- Xenophobic violence (frustration directed at immigrants)\n- Pressure on the social safety net\n- Intergenerational poverty (children of unemployed parents face worse outcomes)\n\nUnemployment costs the SA economy an estimated **R400 billion per year** in lost production."),
  q(7, "Which type of unemployment is most prevalent and most difficult to solve in South Africa?",
    ["Frictional unemployment", "Seasonal unemployment", "Structural unemployment", "Voluntary unemployment"], 2,
    "Structural unemployment is the most prevalent and difficult to solve in SA because it requires fundamental changes to the education system, skills training, and economic structure. It cannot be resolved simply by economic growth."),
  t(8, "### Approaches to Solving Unemployment\n\nSA uses various strategies to combat unemployment:\n\n**Government programmes:**\n- **Expanded Public Works Programme (EPWP)**: Short-term government-funded jobs in infrastructure, social, and environmental projects\n- **Community Works Programme (CWP)**: Part-time work opportunities in local communities\n- **Youth Employment Service (YES)**: Paid work experience for young people\n- **National Youth Development Agency (NYDA)**: Skills training and entrepreneur support for youth\n\n**Education and skills development:**\n- SETAs provide sector-specific training\n- TVET colleges offer technical and vocational education\n- NSFAS funds higher education for poor students\n- Learnerships and apprenticeships combine work and study\n\n**Economic growth strategies:**\n- NDP targets 5-6% GDP growth\n- Investment in infrastructure creates construction jobs\n- Supporting small business and entrepreneurship (SEDA, SEFA)\n- Special Economic Zones to attract investment\n- Industrial policy to support labour-intensive manufacturing\n\n**Labour market reforms:**\n- Reducing red tape for small businesses\n- Youth employment tax incentive (subsidy for hiring young workers)\n- Addressing the skills mismatch through curriculum reform"),
  fb(9, "The EPWP creates ___-term government-funded jobs. SETAs provide sector-specific skills ___.",
    ["short", "training"],
    "The EPWP provides temporary employment relief, while SETAs aim to address structural unemployment through skills development aligned with industry needs."),
  q(10, "Which of the following would help reduce structural unemployment?",
    ["Lowering interest rates", "Increasing social grants", "Improving education quality and aligning skills training with market needs", "Raising the minimum wage"], 2,
    "Structural unemployment is caused by a skills mismatch. The solution is to improve education quality and ensure that training programmes produce the skills that employers actually need. Interest rates address cyclical unemployment; grants address poverty but not unemployment."),
];

