blockNum = 0;
const ch11_lesson1 = [
  t(1, "## Economic Redress and Reconstruction\n\nSouth Africa's economy carries the legacy of apartheid, which systematically excluded the majority of the population from economic participation. **Economic redress** refers to policies aimed at correcting these historical injustices and creating a more inclusive economy.\n\n### The Legacy of Apartheid\n\n**Economic effects of apartheid:**\n- Black South Africans were denied quality education (Bantu Education Act)\n- Restricted from owning land in most of the country (Natives Land Act, Group Areas Act)\n- Excluded from skilled occupations (job reservation)\n- Denied access to capital and entrepreneurship opportunities\n- Forced to live in townships and homelands far from economic centres\n\n**The result:**\n- Extreme inequality (Gini coefficient of 0.63)\n- Racial wealth gap: White households earn approximately 3-4 times more than Black households\n- High unemployment, poverty, and poor living conditions for the majority\n- Underdeveloped infrastructure in former homelands and townships\n- Skills deficit due to inferior education"),
  t(2, "### Factors of Production and Redistribution\n\n**Redress and reconstruction** aim to give previously disadvantaged groups better access to all factors of production:\n\n**Land:**\n- Land reform programme with three pillars:\n  1. **Restitution**: Returning land (or compensation) to those dispossessed after 1913\n  2. **Redistribution**: Transferring land from white to black ownership\n  3. **Tenure reform**: Securing land rights for people on communal or farm land\n- Progress has been slow — less than 10% of commercial farmland has been redistributed\n\n**Labour:**\n- Employment Equity Act promotes fair representation\n- Skills development through SETAs and TVET colleges\n- NSFAS provides financial aid for higher education\n- EPWP and CWP create temporary employment\n\n**Capital:**\n- Black Business Supplier Development Programme\n- Small Enterprise Finance Agency (SEFA) provides loans to black entrepreneurs\n- Industrial Development Corporation (IDC) supports industrial development\n- National Empowerment Fund (NEF) provides finance for BBBEE deals\n\n**Entrepreneurship:**\n- SEDA (Small Enterprise Development Agency) provides business support\n- Business incubators and technology hubs\n- Preferential procurement (government buys from BBBEE-compliant companies)"),
  q(3, "The three pillars of South Africa's land reform programme are:",
    ["Taxation, subsidies, and regulation", "Restitution, redistribution, and tenure reform", "Education, health, and housing", "Imports, exports, and investment"], 1,
    "Land reform consists of restitution (returning land taken after 1913), redistribution (transferring land to disadvantaged people), and tenure reform (securing land rights). Progress has been disappointingly slow."),
  t(4, "### Democratisation of Economic Processes\n\nSince 1994, SA has worked to democratise economic decision-making and ensure broader participation:\n\n**Labour legislation:**\n- Labour Relations Act protects workers' rights\n- Collective bargaining through Bargaining Councils\n- NEDLAC enables consultation between government, labour, business, and community\n\n**Public hearing and participation:**\n- National Budget is debated in Parliament\n- Policy proposals go through public comment periods\n- Community participation in local government budgets (Integrated Development Plans)\n\n**Self-governing regulatory bodies:**\n- Competition Commission: Prevents monopolies and cartels\n- National Consumer Commission: Protects consumer rights\n- Financial Sector Conduct Authority: Regulates financial services\n- Council for Medical Schemes: Regulates medical aids\n\n**Majorification:**\n- Ensuring previously marginalised groups have a greater say in economic decisions\n- BBBEE codes require companies to include black people in ownership, management, and skills development\n- Procurement policies favour BBBEE-compliant businesses\n- Employment Equity targets increase representation in the workplace"),
  fb(5, "The ___ Commission prevents monopolies and anti-competitive behaviour. BBBEE stands for Broad-Based Black ___ Empowerment.",
    ["Competition", "Economic"],
    "The Competition Commission ensures fair competition in markets. BBBEE aims to transform the economy so that previously disadvantaged South Africans can participate more fully."),
  t(6, "### Macro-Economic Adaptations\n\nGovernment uses macroeconomic policies to address inequality and promote redress:\n\n**Fiscal policy:**\n- **Progressive taxation**: Higher income earners pay a higher percentage in tax (18% to 45%)\n- **Government spending**: Prioritises education, health, social grants, and infrastructure\n- **Social grants**: Redistribute income to the poorest households\n\n**Monetary policy:**\n- SARB manages interest rates and inflation to create stable conditions for growth\n- Inflation targeting (3-6%) protects the purchasing power of the poor\n- Stable currency supports trade and investment\n\n**Trade policy:**\n- AGOA gives SA preferential access to US markets\n- AfCFTA opens up African markets for SA goods\n- Trade agreements create export opportunities\n\n**Key indicators for tracking redress:**\n\n| Indicator | What it measures |\n|-----------|------------------|\n| Gini coefficient | Income inequality |\n| Unemployment rate | Job creation progress |\n| HDI | Overall development |\n| Poverty headcount | Proportion living in poverty |\n| BBBEE scorecard | Transformation in business |"),
  q(7, "Progressive taxation means that:",
    ["Everyone pays the same percentage of income in tax", "Higher income earners pay a higher percentage in tax", "The poor pay more tax than the rich", "Tax rates decrease as income increases"], 1,
    "Progressive taxation means the tax rate increases as income increases. In SA, those earning more pay a higher percentage (up to 45%). This redistributes income from richer to poorer households through government spending."),
  t(8, "### Economic Performance, Employment, and Stability\n\nThe success of economic redress policies is measured by their impact on:\n\n**Economic performance:**\n- GDP growth rate (target: 5-6% as per NDP)\n- Investment levels (target: 30% of GDP)\n- Budget balance (revenue vs spending)\n- National debt levels (approximately 74% of GDP — a concern)\n\n**Employment:**\n- Unemployment rate (still above 32%)\n- Youth unemployment (above 60%)\n- Informal sector employment\n- Quality of jobs created\n\n**Income inequality:**\n- Gini coefficient remains among the highest globally\n- Racial income gap has narrowed slightly but remains wide\n- Growth of the black middle class\n- Persistent poverty in rural areas and townships\n\n**Poverty:**\n- Upper bound poverty line: approximately R1 558 per person per month\n- Over 55% of South Africans live below this line\n- Social grants are the main source of income for many households\n- Multi-dimensional poverty includes lack of access to services, not just income\n\n**Stability:**\n- Political stability and democratic institutions\n- Rule of law and property rights\n- Investor confidence (affected by corruption scandals and policy uncertainty)\n- Social cohesion (affected by inequality, xenophobia, and service delivery failures)"),
  fb(9, "SA's Gini coefficient of approximately ___ indicates very high income inequality. Over ___% of South Africans live below the upper bound poverty line.",
    ["0.63", "55"],
    "Despite significant government spending on redistribution (grants, free services), SA remains one of the most unequal countries in the world. Addressing this requires both economic growth and better distribution of its benefits."),
  q(10, "Which of the following best describes the purpose of economic redress in South Africa?",
    ["To make SA a communist country", "To correct historical injustices and create a more inclusive economy", "To eliminate all private enterprise", "To stop all immigration"], 1,
    "Economic redress aims to correct the legacy of apartheid by giving previously disadvantaged groups better access to factors of production, opportunities, and economic participation. It does not aim to eliminate private enterprise but to make the economy more inclusive."),
];

