blockNum = 0;
const ch12_lesson1 = [
  t(1, "## Revision: Key Concepts and Definitions\n\n### Term 1 Concepts\n\n**Basic Economic Concepts:**\n- Scarcity: unlimited wants, limited resources\n- Choice: must decide how to allocate scarce resources\n- Opportunity cost: value of the next best alternative forgone\n- Factors of production: land (rent), labour (wages), capital (interest), entrepreneurship (profit)\n- Positive vs normative economics\n- Free goods vs economic goods\n\n**Circular Flow:**\n- Participants: households, firms, government, foreign sector, financial sector\n- Factor market and goods market\n- Real flows vs money flows\n- Injections: I + G + X\n- Leakages: S + T + M\n- Equilibrium: injections = leakages\n- GDP = GDI = GDE = C + I + G + (X - M)\n\n**Business Cycles:**\n- Four phases: upswing, peak, downswing, trough\n- Recession: two consecutive quarters of negative real GDP growth\n- Leading, coincident, and lagging indicators\n- Endogenous vs exogenous causes\n- Multiplier: k = 1 / (1 - MPC) = 1 / MPS"),
  t(2, "### Term 2 Concepts\n\n**Dynamics of the Market:**\n- Demand: law of demand (inverse relationship between price and quantity demanded)\n- Supply: law of supply (direct relationship between price and quantity supplied)\n- Equilibrium: where demand equals supply\n- Surplus: price above equilibrium\n- Shortage: price below equilibrium\n- Movement along vs shift of curves\n- Perfect vs imperfect markets (monopoly, oligopoly, monopolistic competition)\n\n**Public Sector Intervention:**\n- Reasons for intervention: public goods, externalities, inequality, stabilisation\n- Indirect taxes: VAT (15%), excise duties, customs duties\n- Subsidies: reduce costs, shift supply right\n- Social grants: old age, child support, disability\n- Maximum prices (price ceiling): below equilibrium, creates shortage\n- Minimum prices (price floor): above equilibrium, creates surplus\n- National minimum wage: R27.58/hour\n\n**Production Possibility Curve:**\n- On the PPC: efficient\n- Inside the PPC: inefficient/unemployment\n- Outside the PPC: unattainable\n- Concave shape: law of increasing opportunity cost\n- Outward shift: economic growth\n- Consumer goods vs capital goods trade-off"),
  t(3, "### Term 3 Concepts\n\n**Growth, Development, and Globalisation:**\n- Economic growth: increase in real GDP (quantitative)\n- Economic development: improvement in quality of life (qualitative)\n- HDI: health, education, income\n- Globalisation: increasing integration of world economies\n- BRICS, AfCFTA, AGOA\n- Savings and investment link\n\n**Population and Labour Force:**\n- SA population: approximately 62 million\n- Demographic transition: 4 stages (SA in Stage 3)\n- Labour force (EAP): employed + unemployed seeking work\n- Narrow unemployment: ~32%\n- Expanded unemployment: ~42%\n- Youth unemployment: over 60%\n- Dependency ratio\n\n**Labour Relations:**\n- Key legislation: LRA, BCEA, EEA, Skills Development Act\n- Trade unions and employer organisations\n- Collective bargaining at plant, sectoral, and national (NEDLAC) levels\n- Dispute resolution: conciliation, mediation, arbitration, Labour Court\n- Strikes and lockouts"),
  t(4, "### Term 4 Concepts\n\n**Unemployment:**\n- Types: structural, cyclical, frictional, seasonal, institutional\n- Causes: skills mismatch, economic downturn, regulations\n- Consequences: poverty, crime, lost GDP, social instability\n- Solutions: EPWP, CWP, YES, SETAs, education reform, economic growth\n\n**Economic Redress:**\n- Legacy of apartheid: inequality, racial wealth gap, skills deficit\n- Land reform: restitution, redistribution, tenure reform\n- BBBEE: broad-based black economic empowerment\n- Progressive taxation: 18% to 45%\n- Social grants: over 18 million recipients\n- Democratisation: Competition Commission, NEDLAC, public participation\n- Gini coefficient: approximately 0.63\n- Poverty: over 55% below upper bound poverty line"),
  q(5, "Match the correct formula: the multiplier (k) equals:",
    ["1 / (1 - MPC)", "MPC / MPS", "GDP / Population", "Exports - Imports"], 0,
    "The multiplier k = 1 / (1 - MPC) = 1 / MPS. If MPC = 0.8, then k = 1/0.2 = 5. This shows how an initial injection is multiplied through successive rounds of spending."),
  t(6, "### Key Formulas to Memorise\n\n| Formula | Meaning |\n|---------|--------|\n| **GDP (expenditure)** = C + I + G + (X - M) | Total spending in the economy |\n| **Equilibrium**: I + G + X = S + T + M | Injections equal leakages |\n| **Multiplier**: k = 1 / (1 - MPC) = 1 / MPS | Spending multiplier |\n| **Unemployment rate** = (Unemployed / EAP) x 100 | Percentage of labour force without work |\n| **GDP growth rate** = ((GDP₁ - GDP₀) / GDP₀) x 100 | Percentage change in real GDP |\n| **Per capita GDP** = GDP / Population | Average GDP per person |\n| **Population growth** = ((Births - Deaths + Net migration) / Population) x 100 | Rate of population change |\n\n### Exam Tips\n\n**Section A (Multiple choice and matching):**\n- Read every option carefully before answering\n- Eliminate obviously wrong answers\n- Watch for words like \"always\" and \"never\" which are often incorrect\n\n**Section B (Data response and short answers):**\n- Quote from source material where possible\n- Use correct economic terminology\n- Show all calculations with formulas\n\n**Section C (Essays):**\n- Introduction, body with headings, conclusion\n- Include SA examples throughout\n- Draw and label diagrams where relevant (supply and demand, PPC, business cycle)\n- Address advantages AND disadvantages if asked to discuss"),
  q(7, "If injections into the circular flow are greater than leakages, the economy will:",
    ["Contract", "Expand", "Stay the same", "Enter a recession"], 1,
    "When injections (I + G + X) exceed leakages (S + T + M), more money is flowing into the economy than is flowing out. This increases total spending, production, and employment — the economy expands."),
  fb(8, "The four factors of production are land, labour, ___, and ___. Their remuneration is rent, wages, interest, and profit respectively.",
    ["capital", "entrepreneurship"],
    "This is the most fundamental concept in economics. Land earns rent, labour earns wages/salaries, capital earns interest, and entrepreneurship earns profit."),
  q(9, "SA's economy operates inside its PPC primarily because of:",
    ["Too much government spending", "High unemployment and inefficient use of resources", "Too many exports", "Low population growth"], 1,
    "SA operates inside its PPC because it does not fully utilise its resources. With over 32% unemployment, millions of workers are idle. Load shedding and corruption also reduce efficiency. Moving to the PPC would increase GDP without needing more resources."),
  t(10, "### Final Revision Checklist\n\nBefore your exam, ensure you can:\n\n**Explain and apply:**\n- The basic economic problem (scarcity, choice, opportunity cost)\n- The circular flow model (all four sectors, injections and leakages)\n- Business cycle phases and their effects\n- Demand and supply, equilibrium, surplus, and shortage\n- Government intervention tools (taxes, subsidies, price controls)\n- The PPC and what different points represent\n- Economic growth vs development\n- Population concepts and the demographic transition\n- Labour relations and key legislation\n- Types and causes of unemployment\n- Economic redress policies\n\n**Draw and interpret:**\n- Demand and supply curves with shifts\n- The business cycle diagram\n- The Production Possibility Curve\n- The circular flow model\n\n**Calculate:**\n- The multiplier (from MPC or MPS)\n- GDP using the expenditure method\n- Unemployment rate\n- GDP growth rate\n- Per capita GDP\n\nGood luck with your exam preparation!"),
];

